Gates: SDD

Purpose:
  Define gate criteria for each workflow stage. A gate is the minimum
  set of conditions that must ALL pass before a stage's output is
  accepted and the chain advances to the next stage.

  A gate is a checkpoint, not a separate stage. Gate failure MUST route
  to the responsible stage for recovery, not skip backward (bax chains/
  chains.sdd -> Gate, Failure, VerificationFailure).


Owns:
  ./

Scope:
  Gate definitions per stage
  Pass/fail criteria
  Gate validation rules
  Gate failure routing


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific flows (bax project/flows.sdd)
  Project-specific chains (bax chains/chains.sdd)
  Engineering skills (bax skills/)
  Task instances (bax tasks/)
  Project architecture (bax project/architecture.sdd)


Gates:

  AN — Analysis Gate:
    Pass:
      - Scope is documented
      - Affected components are identified
      - Dependencies are listed
      - Risk level is assigned
      - Impact matrix is defined
    Fail:
      - Missing scope or requirements
      - Unidentified dependencies
      - Routing: AR (architecture clarification) or HUMAN (requirement ambiguity)

  AR — Architecture Gate:
    Pass:
      - Architecture decisions are recorded
      - Module boundaries are defined
      - Dependency direction is validated
      - Security boundaries are identified
      - No circular dependencies
    Fail:
      - Architecture conflicts
      - Circular dependencies
      - Routing: AR (rework) or HUMAN (architecture decision required)

  BDD — BDD Gate:
    Pass:
      - Acceptance criteria are defined in Given/When/Then format
      - All scenarios are covered
      - Edge cases are identified
      - BDD artifacts are linked to task
    Fail:
      - Missing acceptance criteria
      - Ambiguous scenarios
      - Routing: AR (requirement clarification) or QA (scenario rewrite)

  ARCH — Architecture Verification Gate:
    Pass:
      - Implementation matches architecture decisions
      - Module boundaries are respected
      - No unauthorized coupling
      - Dependency direction is correct
    Fail:
      - Architecture violation
      - Unauthorized coupling
      - Routing: BE/FE/MD/API (rework) or AR (architecture change)

  DB — Database Gate:
    Pass:
      - Schema is valid and reviewed
      - Migrations are reversible
      - Indexes are justified
      - Data integrity constraints are defined
      - Rollback plan exists
    Fail:
      - Invalid schema
      - Missing migration
      - Routing: DB (rework) or AR (schema change requires architecture review)

  BE — Backend Gate:
    Pass:
      - Code compiles/builds
      - Unit tests pass
      - Lint passes
      - Error handling is implemented
      - API contract is implemented
      - Security baseline passes (SAST)
    Fail:
      - Compile error
      - Test failure
      - Security finding
      - Routing: BE (fix), SC (security), QA (test gap)

  API — API Gate:
    Pass:
      - Contract is defined and versioned
      - Contract tests pass
      - Breaking changes are documented
      - Backward compatibility is maintained (or migration path exists)
    Fail:
      - Contract mismatch
      - Missing contract tests
      - Routing: API (fix), BE (implementation mismatch)

  FE — Frontend Gate:
    Pass:
      - UI builds successfully
      - Component tests pass
      - Accessibility checks pass
      - Responsive design validated
      - API integration works
    Fail:
      - Build failure
      - Accessibility violation
      - Routing: FE (fix), API (contract issue)

  MD — Mobile Gate:
    Pass:
      - App builds successfully
      - Platform tests pass
      - Offline behavior validated
      - API integration works
      - Platform guidelines followed
    Fail:
      - Build failure
      - Platform violation
      - Routing: MD (fix), API (contract issue)

  QA — Quality Assurance Gate:
    Pass:
      - Unit tests pass
      - Integration tests pass
      - E2E tests pass (if applicable)
      - No critical/high bugs open
      - Code coverage meets threshold
      - Performance tests pass (if applicable)
    Fail:
      - Test failure
      - Bug found
      - Routing: responsible stage (BE/FE/MD/DB/API) for fix, or QA (test gap)

  SC — Security Gate:
    Pass:
      - No CRITICAL or HIGH findings open
      - MEDIUM/LOW findings are tracked or accepted
      - Threat model validated (if applicable)
      - Dependency scan clean
      - Secrets not exposed
      - AuthZ/authN validated
    Fail:
      - CRITICAL/HIGH finding open
      - Security baseline violation
      - Routing: responsible stage (BE/FE/MD/DB/API/AR) for fix, or HUMAN (security exception)

  DO — DevOps Gate:
    Pass:
      - Build succeeds
      - Deployment succeeds
      - Health checks pass
      - Rollback plan is tested
      - Monitoring is configured
      - Infrastructure as code is validated
    Fail:
      - Deployment failure
      - Infrastructure issue
      - Routing: DO (fix), BE/FE/MD (application issue)

  VR — Verification Gate:
    Pass:
      - All required stages passed
      - All gates passed
      - Documentation is current
      - DRIFT check passes
      - Security findings are resolved or accepted
      - Business acceptance criteria met
    Fail:
      - Missing stage output
      - Documentation stale
      - Security finding unresolved
      - Routing: responsible stage for rework, or HUMAN (acceptance decision)


GateFailure:

  Gate failure MUST route to FailureRouting (bax tasks/tasks.sdd ->
  FailureRouting). The chain MUST NOT advance past an ungated stage.

  Routing table:
    code issue      -> responsible implementation stage
    architecture    -> AR
    database        -> DB
    security        -> SC (finding) + responsible stage (fix)
    test gap        -> QA
    infrastructure  -> DO
    requirement     -> HUMAN


GateCriteria:

  Every gate criterion MUST be:
    - Measurable (pass/fail, not subjective)
    - Automated where possible
    - Documented with its evidence source
    - Traceable to a requirement or risk


Rules:

  [G1]  Every stage MUST have a gate defined in this file.

  [G2]  A gate MUST declare at least one pass criterion and one fail
        criterion.

  [G3]  ALL pass criteria MUST be satisfied before the stage is
        considered complete.

  [G4]  ANY fail criterion MUST block advancement and route to
        FailureRouting.

  [G5]  Gate criteria MUST be measurable — subjective criteria MUST NOT
        be used as pass/fail gates.

  [G6]  Gate criteria MAY be automated (test, lint, scan) or manual
        (review, architecture check), but manual gates MUST be
        explicitly marked as such.

  [G7]  A stage MUST NOT be marked complete without its gate passing.

  [G8]  Gate failure MUST route to the responsible owner, not to the
        next stage.

  [G9]  Security Gate (SC) failure at CRITICAL or HIGH MUST block VR
        regardless of other gate results (bax chains/chains.sdd ->
        SecurityGate, tasks/tasks.sdd -> SecuritySeverity).

  [G10] VR Gate failure MUST trigger impact analysis before any
        backward transition (bax chains/chains.sdd -> VerificationFailure,
        transitions.sdd -> [TR10]).

  [G11] A gate MAY be waived only by human decision (bax PROJECT.sdd ->
        HumanDecision). AI MUST NOT waive a gate.

  [G12] Gate criteria are defined at the system level here — project-
        specific additions belong in project/flows.sdd or project/<domain>/
        flows.sdd, not here (bax DoesNotOwn).


